WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.567
Your move.

00:00:00.567 --> 00:00:02.102
Governor DeSantis.

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:03.636
I'm Michael Smerconish in Philadelphia.

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:05.672
Friday, Attorney General Merrick Garland

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:07.407
appointed a special counsel

00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:09.476
regarding the federal investigations

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:11.011
into the events of January six

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:12.412
and Donald Trump's holding on

00:00:12.412 --> 00:00:14.814
to presidential records at Mar a Lago.

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:17.751
Here's how he explained the move

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:19.953
based on recent developments

00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:20.954
including the former

00:00:20.954 --> 00:00:22.222
president's announcement

00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:23.156
that he is a candidate

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:25.458
for president in the next election.

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:26.659
And the sitting president

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:27.961
stated intention

00:00:27.961 --> 00:00:29.996
to be a candidate as well.

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:31.798
I have concluded that it is

00:00:31.798 --> 00:00:33.333
in the public interest

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:36.002
to appoint a special counsel.

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:36.302
Trump

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:38.505
then responded during a keynote speech

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:39.172
at the America

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:40.640
First Gala at Mar a Lago,

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.509
calling it an appalling announcement

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:44.511
and a horrendous abuse of power.

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:47.280
They want to do bad things

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.581
to the greatest movement

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:51.851
in the history of our country,

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:54.187
but in particular bad things to me.

00:00:54.888 --> 00:00:56.856
But I've gotten used to it.

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:57.457
It's lucky.

00:00:58.491 --> 00:00:59.192
It's lucky.

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:00.660
A lot of people wouldn't get used to it

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.095
so easily.

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:05.832
This is a rigged deal, just as the 2020

00:01:05.832 --> 00:01:06.866
election was rigged,

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:10.203
and we can't let them get away with it.

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:11.471
So what could all of this mean

00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:12.739
for the 2024

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:14.641
race and Trump's potential leading

00:01:14.641 --> 00:01:16.576
rival, Ron DeSantis?

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:17.944
Trump had just made his intention

00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:18.311
to run

00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:19.546
official Tuesday night,

00:01:19.546 --> 00:01:20.547
just one week

00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:22.115
after an underwhelming performance

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:24.484
by Republicans in the midterms.

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:27.020
One bright spot for the GOP the 20 point

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.054
landslide victory

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:30.123
by Florida Governor Ron DeSantis

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:32.225
that despite Trump nicknaming him

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:34.294
Ron de sanctimonious,

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:35.995
that was a shot across DeSantis

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:36.663
Bow, a

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:37.931
warning of the rough

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:38.765
and tumble world

00:01:38.765 --> 00:01:41.034
of being a Donald Trump opponent.

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.503
Soon, DeSantis is going to have to decide

00:01:43.503 --> 00:01:45.071
what he wants to do.

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:46.206
He's only 44.

00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:47.006
The question is

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:48.241
whether the next election

00:01:48.241 --> 00:01:49.709
is his best shot.

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:52.245
You know, strike while the iron is hot.

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:53.947
Ask any Paul and they'll cite

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:55.415
two examples of candidates

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:57.150
at similar crossroads

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.918
with vastly different outcomes.

00:01:58.918 --> 00:01:59.652
Barack Obama

00:01:59.652 --> 00:02:02.322
in 2008 Chris Christie in 2012.

00:02:02.856 --> 00:02:04.424
Remember many scoffed

00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:05.959
at the junior senator from Illinois

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:06.893
when he announced

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:08.294
his presidential campaign

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:09.963
shortly after being elected to the U.S.

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:10.697
Senate.

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:13.133
But Obama caught lightning in a bottle

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:15.301
and the rest is history.

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:16.803
Then there's Chris Christie

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:17.570
the Tough-Talking

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.005
former prosecutor was elected

00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:20.306
governor of New Jersey

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:21.541
in an upstart campaign

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:25.111
against incumbent Jon Corzine in 2009.

00:02:25.445 --> 00:02:27.614
Christie was Trump before Trump.

00:02:27.614 --> 00:02:28.915
Relishing his boardwalk

00:02:28.915 --> 00:02:30.350
face to face confrontations

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.485
with Garden State detractors

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:33.753
while Christie contemplated

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:35.622
running for president in 2012

00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:37.290
against Obama.

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.158
His decision, of course

00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:39.826
came before the reported hug

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:41.060
between the two

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.163
at the time of Superstorm Sandy.

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:45.231
Instead, Christie opted to burnish

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:46.933
his gubernatorial credentials

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:47.934
while running up the score

00:02:47.934 --> 00:02:49.269
in his reelection.

00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:51.070
Christie was reelected,

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.839
but his fortunes quickly turned.

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:54.607
Anyone remember the George W?

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:55.808
The George Washington Bridge

00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:57.310
closure scandal?

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.811
Soon after morning, Consult

00:02:58.811 --> 00:02:59.179
found that

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:00.513
Christie had the worst disapproval

00:03:00.513 --> 00:03:02.182
of any American governor.

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:03.483
By his last day in office,

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:05.552
his approval rating stood at 14%

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:07.787
And when he did eventually run

00:03:07.787 --> 00:03:09.923
for the presidency in 2016

00:03:09.923 --> 00:03:10.423
he placed

00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:12.325
sixth in the New Hampshire primary

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:14.260
and then dropped out of the race.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:16.863
No doubt DeSantis is studying the Obama

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:18.331
and Christie playbooks

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:19.165
and trying to figure out

00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:20.667
which model matches

00:03:20.667 --> 00:03:22.202
his current situation.

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:23.670
And DeSantis calculus

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:24.871
is complicated by the presence

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:26.072
of Donald Trump.

00:03:26.072 --> 00:03:26.973
Early polling

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.741
commissioned by the Club for Growth

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:30.009
shows that DeSantis

00:03:30.009 --> 00:03:30.910
handily beats

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:33.213
Trump in critical early states.

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:34.881
DeSantis leads Trump by 11

00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:36.349
percentage points in Iowa

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:38.618
by 15 points in New Hampshire.

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:39.018
He leads

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:40.587
Trump by 26 points in Florida

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:42.789
and 20 points in Georgia.

00:03:42.789 --> 00:03:45.091
But that's head to head with Trump.

00:03:45.625 --> 00:03:46.659
And there's no guarantee

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.661
that DeSantis gets Trump alone

00:03:48.661 --> 00:03:50.096
one on one.

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:51.698
Remember back in 2016,

00:03:51.698 --> 00:03:53.199
Trump was able to win the nomination

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:55.201
because he had a relatively small

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:57.203
but loyal group of supporters

00:03:57.203 --> 00:03:59.472
at a very crowded debate stage.

00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:01.608
I remember being at the Reagan Library

00:04:01.608 --> 00:04:02.709
for the second GOP

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:04.677
debate of the 2016 cycle

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:07.113
There were 15 candidates,

00:04:07.113 --> 00:04:08.815
so many on that debate stage

00:04:08.815 --> 00:04:11.017
that it had to be split in two.

00:04:11.017 --> 00:04:12.885
The more the merrier for Donald Trump,

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:14.954
but not for Ron DeSantis.

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:15.822
As he decides

00:04:15.822 --> 00:04:17.757
whether to run for president in 2024.

00:04:17.757 --> 00:04:19.626
DeSantis has to be worried about more

00:04:19.626 --> 00:04:21.561
than just the size of the field.

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:24.130
The only venue for a fight with Donald

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.566
Trump is a steel cage.

00:04:26.566 --> 00:04:28.201
DeSantis might win the battle

00:04:28.201 --> 00:04:29.168
and lose the war.

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:30.136
There's no guarantee

00:04:30.136 --> 00:04:32.905
that if you beat Trump, he embraces you.

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:34.741
Trump's for Trump.

00:04:34.741 --> 00:04:36.876
Not for a party. Not for an ideology.

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:38.344
It's hard to envision

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:39.779
DeSantis vanquishing

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:40.613
Trump,

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:41.547
but still gaining

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:43.516
his endorsement thereafter.

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:45.752
Remember Trump's a guy who trashed

00:04:45.752 --> 00:04:46.919
presidential protocol

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:47.854
and refused to attend

00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:49.856
Joe Biden's inauguration.

00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:52.091
He relishes burning down the house.

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:52.592
Just ask

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:53.493
Georgia senators

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:55.795
David Perdue and Kelly Loeffler.

00:04:55.795 --> 00:04:57.230
So DeSantis has to consider

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:58.698
that he could enter the race,

00:04:58.698 --> 00:05:00.767
get Trump in a small field and beat him.

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:02.302
Big ifs

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:04.103
only to have Trump trash him

00:05:04.103 --> 00:05:06.072
after the nomination is settled

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:06.673
and tank

00:05:06.673 --> 00:05:07.640
the general election

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.609
to the benefit of Democrats.

00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:11.711
Maybe the better course is to sit it out

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:13.579
and wait for another cycle

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:15.181
Or maybe he should call Chris Christie.

00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:17.717
Joining me now to discuss is Gary

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:20.286
Final, who writes the Florida playbook

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:21.988
for Politico.

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:24.157
Gary also just wrote this piece.

00:05:24.157 --> 00:05:24.557
Gary,

00:05:24.557 --> 00:05:25.358
what are your thoughts

00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:27.093
in terms of how the Merrick Garland

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:30.029
announcement impacts DeSantis timeline?

00:05:31.931 --> 00:05:33.466
Well, I'm not really sure it will.

00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:34.200
I mean, obviously,

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:34.667
there's a lot

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:35.368
going on there

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:38.671
and we'll see where all that plays out.

00:05:38.671 --> 00:05:40.540
It's my understanding that DeSantis,

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:43.876
for the most part, the timeline is if

00:05:43.876 --> 00:05:45.311
it is when a decision is made,

00:05:45.311 --> 00:05:46.979
it would be made sometime

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:49.148
probably in the late spring

00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:50.850
after the next legislative session

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.251
here in Florida.

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:53.553
So I'm not sure this is going

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:56.022
to immediately impact that timeline.

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:57.423
They've got a transition to a deal

00:05:57.423 --> 00:05:59.392
where they've got an inauguration

00:05:59.392 --> 00:06:00.526
and then sort of the

00:06:00.526 --> 00:06:01.561
the upcoming session

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:02.061
in which

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:04.163
I would anticipate that

00:06:04.697 --> 00:06:06.165
the Florida legislature will again

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:08.735
be called upon to do things to sort of,

00:06:08.835 --> 00:06:09.802
you know, help,

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:10.970
you know, brandish his name

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:13.306
you have written that

00:06:13.306 --> 00:06:14.707
all signs

00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:17.043
make it appear likely that he does run.

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:19.779
What's the basis for that opinion?

00:06:19.779 --> 00:06:21.914
Well, I think item

00:06:21.914 --> 00:06:22.615
number one

00:06:22.615 --> 00:06:25.985
is he's sitting on a lot of money.

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:27.854
He he raised a probably

00:06:27.854 --> 00:06:28.921
what may be a record

00:06:28.921 --> 00:06:31.991
for a gubernatorial candidate in America.

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:33.693
And he basically

00:06:33.693 --> 00:06:35.895
they didn't have to spend all of it.

00:06:35.928 --> 00:06:37.163
I think that

00:06:37.163 --> 00:06:37.997
last count probably

00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:40.566
about 70 to 80 million that

00:06:41.033 --> 00:06:42.335
And I know there are some questions

00:06:42.335 --> 00:06:43.870
as to legally how you do all this

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:45.438
and the hurdles and everything like that.

00:06:45.438 --> 00:06:46.706
But 70 80 million

00:06:46.706 --> 00:06:47.440
that could be transferred

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:48.808
over to a super PAC

00:06:48.808 --> 00:06:50.676
at some point in time.

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:52.178
That's item number one.

00:06:52.178 --> 00:06:52.478
You know,

00:06:52.478 --> 00:06:54.113
and then when we talk about the timeline,

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:55.248
you were talking about the whole Christie

00:06:55.248 --> 00:06:56.282
Obama thing.

00:06:56.282 --> 00:06:57.750
I understand that,

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.318
you know, he just got reelected.

00:06:59.318 --> 00:07:01.587
So let's just do the math.

00:07:01.587 --> 00:07:02.855
We have term limits in Florida.

00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:06.426
So basically he would leave office in 26.

00:07:06.426 --> 00:07:07.493
So if he

00:07:07.493 --> 00:07:10.029
if he waited, he would be out of office.

00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:11.164
For two years.

00:07:11.164 --> 00:07:12.565
And, you know, the thing about it

00:07:12.565 --> 00:07:14.400
is, is that having

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:15.134
the governorship

00:07:15.134 --> 00:07:17.336
is a good place to get a lot of attention

00:07:17.637 --> 00:07:19.272
and media attention

00:07:19.272 --> 00:07:20.773
and in other things like that.

00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:23.509
So you just wonder how we how

00:07:23.509 --> 00:07:23.843
it would be.

00:07:23.843 --> 00:07:24.777
I mean, a thing about,

00:07:24.777 --> 00:07:25.044
you know,

00:07:25.044 --> 00:07:25.678
Jeb Bush,

00:07:25.678 --> 00:07:26.479
he was one of the ones

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:28.915
who was vanquished by Trump.

00:07:28.915 --> 00:07:30.383
He'd been office out of office

00:07:30.383 --> 00:07:31.417
for about eight years.

00:07:31.417 --> 00:07:33.453
And even though he raised a lot of money

00:07:33.453 --> 00:07:34.720
for that race, it's

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:35.288
sort of like

00:07:35.288 --> 00:07:36.722
he had been out of the picture.

00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:37.890
Now, two years is

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:39.659
obviously different than eight, but

00:07:39.659 --> 00:07:41.194
but it's it's something to consider.

00:07:42.562 --> 00:07:44.363
He hasn't taken the bait so far.

00:07:44.363 --> 00:07:44.697
Right.

00:07:44.697 --> 00:07:45.698
The sanctimonious.

00:07:45.698 --> 00:07:47.533
There was no respond in kind.

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:49.769
Does that surprise you?

00:07:49.769 --> 00:07:50.403
No.

00:07:50.403 --> 00:07:52.972
No, I think well, I

00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:55.441
we had a story a few days ago

00:07:55.441 --> 00:07:57.109
in which we quoted somebody,

00:07:57.109 --> 00:07:57.677
a consultant,

00:07:57.677 --> 00:07:58.845
who said there's

00:07:58.845 --> 00:07:59.946
there's sort of a thinking about,

00:07:59.946 --> 00:08:02.381
well, maybe Donald will punch itself out

00:08:02.381 --> 00:08:03.483
that you know,

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:04.851
they're not going to respond.

00:08:04.851 --> 00:08:06.552
Now, you did see that he's taken

00:08:06.552 --> 00:08:08.354
sort of these

00:08:08.588 --> 00:08:10.523
these he's made comments

00:08:10.523 --> 00:08:11.757
without responding

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:13.693
directly to the insults.

00:08:13.693 --> 00:08:14.894
He's basically said,

00:08:14.894 --> 00:08:16.562
you know, just the other day

00:08:16.562 --> 00:08:17.530
when somebody asked him

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:19.098
point blank about Trump,

00:08:19.098 --> 00:08:20.199
he said something about,

00:08:20.199 --> 00:08:21.701
well, you know, I'm going to focus on

00:08:21.701 --> 00:08:22.568
what I'm doing, but,

00:08:22.568 --> 00:08:23.336
you know, what we're doing in

00:08:23.336 --> 00:08:24.036
Florida is working.

00:08:24.036 --> 00:08:25.671
Look at the scoreboard.

00:08:25.671 --> 00:08:27.240
And he was telling in another time,

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:27.840
he said, you know what,

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:28.674
people just need to chill

00:08:28.674 --> 00:08:29.675
out about all of this. So

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:32.044
I think that he

00:08:32.044 --> 00:08:33.446
you know, I think that they're sort of

00:08:33.446 --> 00:08:35.248
in a standpoint where,

00:08:35.248 --> 00:08:35.481
you know,

00:08:35.481 --> 00:08:36.983
they've just come off a very large

00:08:36.983 --> 00:08:37.850
win, a win.

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:38.251
They gave him

00:08:38.251 --> 00:08:40.920
super majorities in the legislature.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:41.787
There's going to be those

00:08:41.787 --> 00:08:44.023
who who feel that the Republican

00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:45.691
takeover of Congress

00:08:45.691 --> 00:08:47.693
is in no small part to Ron DeSantis

00:08:47.693 --> 00:08:49.328
because it was his administration

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:50.630
that pushed forward a map

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:52.498
that netted Republicans

00:08:52.498 --> 00:08:54.700
four seats in Congress.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:56.802
Now, that map is being litigated

00:08:56.903 --> 00:08:57.403
and there's those

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:59.138
who say it is unconstitutional.

00:08:59.138 --> 00:09:00.406
But we'll see.

00:09:00.406 --> 00:09:01.374
But for now,

00:09:01.374 --> 00:09:02.875
he's got all these wins

00:09:02.875 --> 00:09:04.710
that he can point to

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:08.080
as he moves into into next year.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:09.115
You're closer to it.

00:09:09.115 --> 00:09:09.749
My hunch

00:09:09.749 --> 00:09:10.149
is that he

00:09:10.149 --> 00:09:11.951
probably wants to keep his powder dry

00:09:11.951 --> 00:09:13.953
and hope that Merrick Garland

00:09:13.986 --> 00:09:15.187
or the special counsel

00:09:15.187 --> 00:09:16.656
do some of his work for him.

00:09:16.656 --> 00:09:17.757
Gary, thank you, as always.

00:09:17.757 --> 00:09:18.991
We appreciate your insights.

